The chain computes `complianceGate` but never sets `g1bManualReview`. The compliance gate result doesn't include a `g1bManualReview` field. So this badge signal is never written.\n\nThe comment in the badge collector says: \"state.g1bManualReview — boolean (retries exhausted)\". This suggests there should be a retry loop for the compliance gate, similar to the G3 and G4 retry loops. But the chain doesn't have a compliance gate retry loop. So `g1bManualReview` is never set.\n\nLet me also look at the G3 gate. The badge collector reads `state?.g3ManualReview`. The v5 description says G3 is \"inline in chain pre-render. Density checks.\" But I don't see any G3 computation in the chain code. The state object doesn't include `g3ManualReview` or `g3_review_gaps`.\n\nSo to summarize the wiring gaps:\n1. `state.complianceGate` — computed but not written to state\n2. `state.g1bManualReview` — never computed or written\n3. `state.cost_reality_*` — never computed or written (G2 missing)\n4. `state.g3ManualReview` — never computed or written (G3 missing)\n5. `state.g3_review_gaps` — never computed or written (G3 missing)\n\nThese are all fields that the renderer or worker reads but the chain doesn't write.\n\nNow for Question 3 (drift-proof), let me think about what guarantees exist:\n\n1. The knip.config.ts defines entry points — this helps detect dead code but doesn't prevent new entry points\n2. There's no shared type contract between chain (writer) and renderer (reader)\n3. The state object is untyped (`any`) in the renderer\n4. No integration test validates state shape\n5. No CI check that worker snapshot fields match chain output\n\nThe chain could drift from the renderer if someone adds a field to one but not the other. The current gaps (complianceGate, cost_reality, g3ManualReview) are evidence that this has already happened.\n\nFor Question 5, let me think about Engine D's env bootstrap:\n\nThe worker loads env from `~/.claude/secrets/openrouter.env`. The chain inherits this env. Engine D inherits the chain's env. So OPENROUTER_API_KEY should be available.\n\nBut the v5 description says Engine D's \"own env bootstrap loads OPENROUTER from /Users/tristanfischer/secrets/openrouter.env (note: NOT ~/.claude/secrets/).\" This suggests Engine D has its own env loading code that looks in a different path.\n\nIf Engine D's bootstrap:\n- Uses the same `if (!process.env[k])` guard → the already-set key from the worker is used, no problem\n- Overwrites the key → could set it to undefined if the file doesn't exist, breaking Engine D\n- Exits fatally if the file doesn't exist → Engine D never runs\n\nWithout seeing Engine D's code, I can't be sure. But the risk is real: if someone runs the chain directly (not via the worker), and Engine D's bootstrap looks in the wrong path, Engine D silently degrades.\n\nActually, the question says \"If that file doesn't exist + the key isn't already in env from worker bootstrap, Engine D silently degrades.\" So the concern is specifically about the case where the key isn't in env. In the worker path, the key IS in env. In the direct-run path, it might not be.\n\nBut the question asks \"Verify whether this matters.\" In production (worker path), it doesn't matter because the worker loads the key. In development (direct run), it could matter if the developer doesn't have the key in their env.\n\nLet me also check: does the worker's `loadEnvFile` for `~/.claude/secrets/openrouter.env` actually work? The path is `resolve(homedir(), '.claude/secrets/openrouter.env')`. On macOS, `homedir()` returns `/Users/tristanfischer`. So the path is `/Users/tristanfischer/.claude/secrets/openrouter.env`. This is different from `/Users/tristanfischer/secrets/openrouter.env` (the path Engine D supposedly uses).\n\nSo the worker loads from `~/.claude/secrets/openrouter.env` (correct path), and Engine D's bootstrap loads from `/Users/tristanfischer/secrets/openrouter.env` (wrong path, no dotfile). If Engine D's bootstrap is the only place the key is loaded, and the file doesn't exist at that path, Engine D would fail when run directly (without the worker).\n\nBut when run via the worker, the key is already in env, so Engine D's bootstrap is irrelevant (assuming it uses the `if (!process.env[k])` guard).\n\nOK, I think I have enough to write a comprehensive response. Let me organize it.\n\nActually, one more thing. Let me re-read the chain code for the state object construction more carefully. I want to make sure I'm not missing any fields.\n\nThe state object is:\n```typescript\nconst state = {\n    projectId: 'chain-v2-' + Date.now(),\n    parsedBrief: parsedResult.data,\n    moduleDecomposition: design,\n    naturalLanguageLayer: nl,\n    briefOverviewProse: design.brief_overview_prose ?? null,\n    keyMetrics: keyMetrics ?? null,\n    brief: briefBlock,\n    designDecisions,\n    partVerifications,\n    partRecommendations,\n    partVerificationSummary: { ... },\n    physicsCritique: critique,\n    physicsLedger,\n    gatesPassed: allPassed,\n    grammarVerdicts: ...,\n    g5ManualReview,\n    g5UnverifiedParts,\n    acceptanceStatus,\n    savedAt: new Date().toISOString(),\n}\n```\n\nMissing fields that are read downstream:\n- `complianceGate` — read by renderer badge collector\n- `g1bManualReview` — read by renderer badge collector\n- `cost_reality` / `cost_reality_rejection` / `cost_reality_status` — read by renderer badge collector + worker snapshot\n- `g3ManualReview` — read by renderer badge collector + worker snapshot\n- `g3_review_gaps` — read by worker snapshot\n- `suppliers` — read by renderer (SuppliersPage at render-minimal-pdf.tsx:4049)\n- `suppliers_provenance` — mentioned in Engine D comment\n\nWait, `suppliers` and `suppliers_provenance` are written by Engine D (which reads state.json, adds fields, writes back). So they would be in state.json after Engine D runs. The renderer reads from the final state.json. So these are fine — they're written by Engine D, not by the chain's initial state construction.\n\nSimilarly, Engine B writes `engine_b_component_class` and price fields, and Engine C writes `engine_c_*` fields. These are all added by the sub-processes that read/modify/write state.json.\n\nBut `complianceGate`, `g1bManualReview`, `cost_reality_*`, `g3ManualReview`, and `g3_review_gaps` are NOT written by any sub-process. They should be written by the chain itself. And they're not in the state object.\n\nActually, wait. Could the chain add these fields AFTER the sub-processes run? The chain writes state.json, then Engine B/C/D modify it, then... does the chain read it back and add more fields? Looking at the code flow:\n\n1. Chain constructs state object → writes state.json\n2. Engine B reads state.json → modifies → writes back\n3. Engine C reads state.json → modifies → writes back\n4. Engine D reads state.json → modifies → writes back\n5. Deployment envelope code reads state.json → modifies → writes back\n6. Renderer reads state.json\n\nBetween steps 4 and 6, could the chain add G2/G3 fields? The deployment envelope code reads state.json and writes it back. It could potentially add fields. But the code shown only adds `deploymentEnvelope`.\n\nSo no, G2 and G3 fields are never added to state.json.\n\nLet me also check: the deployment envelope code reads `liveState` from state.json and writes it back. Does it add `complianceGate`? No — it only adds `deploymentEnvelope`.\n\nOK, I'm confident in my findings. Let me write the response.\n\nWait, I should also check one more thing. The chain code has this section after the compliance gate:\n\n```typescript\n    why_it_matters: tmpl.why_it_matters,\n    recommendation: tmpl.recommendation,\n    recommended_value: 'Human engineer review required',\n    generated_by: 'structural-gate-router',\n    generated_at: new Date().toISOString(),\n  } as DesignDecision)\n  surfacedFromGates++\n}\n```\n\nThis is the structural-gate router that surfaces unrepaired gates as design decisions. This code is truncated — I can't see the full context. But it seems to be routing structural gate failures to design decisions, not writing compliance gate results to state.\n\nAnd then:\n```typescript\nif (surfacedFromGates > 0) {\n    console.error(`[chain] structural-gate router: surfaced ${surfacedFromGates} unrepaired gate(s) as design decisions`)\n    writeFileSync(resolve(outDir, '10-design-decisions.json'), JSON.stringify(designDecisions, null, 2))\n}\nlogAction({ step: 'structural_gate_routing', count: surfacedFromGates })\n```\n\nThis writes design decisions to a separate file, not to state.json.\n\nOK, final check: the `g4ManualReview` field. The chain sets `(design as any).g4ManualReview = true` on the `design` object (which is `moduleDecomposition`). The state object includes `moduleDecomposition: design`. So `state.moduleDecomposition.g4ManualReview` would be `true` when G4 fails. The badge collector reads `state?.moduleDecomposition?.g4ManualReview === true`. This is correctly wired.\n\nBut `g1bManualReview` is never set on any object. The badge collector reads `state?.g1bManualReview`. This is a gap.\n\nAlright, let me write the final response now.\n\nLet me also think about whether there are any silent failure modes introduced by v5.\n\n1. The compliance gate is wrapped in try/catch and failures are logged but the chain continues. If `complianceGate` is null (due to an exception), the badge collector checks `const cg = state?.complianceGate` which would be undefined (since complianceGate isn't written to state at all). So the G1b badge never fires, regardless of whether the compliance gate succeeds or fails. This is a silent failure — the compliance check runs but its results are invisible.\n\n2. The physics ledger is also wrapped in try/catch. But `physicsLedger` IS written to state (as `physicsLedger: physicsLedger`). If the ledger fails, `physicsLedger` is null, and the badge collector checks `if (pl && (pl.verdict === 'WARN' || pl.verdict === 'HALT'))` — which would be false for null. So the G0 badge correctly doesn't fire when the ledger fails. This is correct behavior.\n\n3. Engine D's failure is caught and the chain continues. If Engine D fails, `state.suppliers` is never populated, and the renderer's SuppliersPage checks `state.suppliers` and skips §7 when empty. This is correct fail-soft behavior.\n\nOK, I think I have a thorough analysis. Let me write it up.\n\nLet me count my findings:\n\n1. complianceGate not written to state (wiring gap)\n2. g1bManualReview never computed or written (wiring gap)\n3. G2 CostRealityGate code missing entirely (wiring gap)\n4. G3 ReviewCompletenessGate code missing entirely (wiring gap)\n5. g3_review_gaps never written (wiring gap)\n6. Worker snapshot reads cost_reality fields that are always null (silent empty diagnostic)\n7. No shared state type contract between chain and renderer (drift risk)\n8. No integration test validating state shape (drift risk)\n9. knip doesn't prevent new entry points (drift risk)\n10. variation_of parent inheritance makes variations impractical (critical deferred item)\n11. Engine D env path risk in direct-run mode (minor, doesn't affect production)\n\nLet me organize these into the 5 question categories.\n\nActually, I realize I should also check: does the chain write `complianceGate` to state.json via a separate write after the sub-processes? Let me look at the code flow again.\n\nThe chain:\n1. Constructs state object (without complianceGate)\n2. Writes state.json\n3. Runs Engine B (reads/modifies/writes state.json)\n4. Runs Engine C (reads/modifies/writes state.json)\n5. Runs Engine D (reads/modifies/writes state.json)\n6. Deployment envelope (reads/modifies/writes state.json)\n7. Runs renderer\n\nBetween steps 6 and 7, is there any code that adds complianceGate? The excerpt is truncated after the deployment envelope code. But based on what's shown, there's no such code.\n\nEven if there were code to add complianceGate after the sub-processes, it would need to read state.json, add the field, and write it back. This","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me carefully audit the v5 changes against the five questions. I need to be forensic and precise, citing file:line for every finding.\n\nLet me go through each question systematically.\n\n## Question 1: Are ALL claimed v5 fixes actually wired correctly into the chain?\n\n### G1b ComplianceGate\n- The file `src/lib/pdf-engine-v2/stages/3.5-compliance-gate.ts` is shown and exports `runComplianceGate` and `ComplianceGateResult`.\n- In the chain (`serial-design-chain-v2.tsx`), I can see the import: `import { runComplianceGate, type ComplianceGateResult } from '../src/lib/pdf-engine-v2/stages/3.5-compliance-gate'`\n- The chain calls `complianceGate = runComplianceGate(parsedResult.data, productClass, currentBriefText)` — this matches the function signature.\n- BUT: The chain writes `complianceGate` to a local variable but I need to check if it's written to `state`. Looking at the state object construction... I see `physicsLedger` is written to state, but I don't see `complianceGate` being written to state in the excerpt provided!\n\nWait, let me re-read the state object. The state object shown includes:\n```\nphysicsCritique: critique,\nphysicsLedger,\ngatesPassed: allPassed,\ngrammarVerdicts: ...\ng5ManualReview,\ng5UnverifiedParts,\nacceptanceStatus,\nsavedAt: ...\n```\n\nI don't see `complianceGate` in the state object! The renderer's `collectManualReviewBadges` reads `state?.complianceGate` (line shown in the badge collector: `const cg = state?.complianceGate`). But the chain never writes `complianceGate` to the state object.\n\nThis is a WIRING GAP. The chain computes `complianceGate` but doesn't persist it to `state.complianceGate`.\n\nActually wait — the state object excerpt might be truncated. Let me look more carefully. The state object shown ends with `savedAt: new Date().toISOString()}`. But the code is clearly truncated (the excerpt shows `...` in various places). However, I need to work with what's shown.\n\nLooking at the state object construction, I see these fields:\n- projectId\n- parsedBrief\n- moduleDecomposition\n- naturalLanguageLayer\n- briefOverviewProse\n- keyMetrics\n- brief\n- designDecisions\n- partVerifications\n- partRecommendations\n- partVerificationSummary\n- physicsCritique\n- physicsLedger\n- gatesPassed\n- grammarVerdicts\n- g5ManualReview\n- g5UnverifiedParts\n- acceptanceStatus\n- savedAt\n\nNo `complianceGate` field! The renderer reads `state?.complianceGate` but the chain never writes it. This is a clear wiring gap.\n\n### G2 CostRealityGate\nThe v5 description says \"G2 CostRealityGate — inline in chain post-Engine-B/C. Computes BoM total, flags <£100 or >£10M. Writes state.cost_reality_*.\"\n\nBut I don't see ANY code for G2 in the provided excerpts. The state object doesn't include `cost_reality_*` fields. The worker's state snapshot reads `parsed.cost_reality?.bom_total_gbp` and `parsed.cost_reality?.verdict`, which suggests the worker expects `state.cost_reality` to exist. But the chain code shown doesn't compute or write it.\n\nWait, the state object might be truncated. But the worker snapshot code explicitly reads:\n```javascript\nbom_total_gbp: parsed.cost_reality?.bom_total_gbp ?? null,\ncost_reality_verdict: parsed.cost_reality?.verdict ?? null,\n```\n\nAnd the badge collector references:\n```\n//   G2  state.cost_reality_rejection                                 — Jaccard reject details (optional)\n//       state.cost_reality_status === 'manual_review_required'       — general re-emit exhaustion\n```\n\nBut I see no code in the chain that computes or writes `cost_reality`, `cost_reality_rejection`, or `cost_reality_status`. This is another wiring gap — G2 is claimed as a v5 fix but the code to compute it is missing from the chain.\n\n### G3 ReviewCompletenessGate\nThe v5 description says \"G3 ReviewCompletenessGate — inline in chain pre-render. Density checks. Writes state.g3ManualReview + state.g3_review_gaps.\"\n\nThe badge collector reads `state?.g3ManualReview` and the worker snapshot reads `parsed.g3ManualReview`. But I don't see any code in the chain that computes or writes `g3ManualReview` or `g3_review_gaps`. The state object shown doesn't include these fields.\n\nAnother wiring gap.\n\n### M6 stale-running recovery\nThe worker code shows both Part A (startup scan) and Part B (per-poll scan). The CAS guard `.eq('status', 'running')` is present. This looks correctly wired.\n\n### M8 state.json + actions.jsonl upload\nThe worker code shows upload of both state.json and actions.jsonl to the storage bucket. The state_snapshot_json column is expanded with diagnostic fields. This looks correctly wired.\n\n### physicsCritique render section\nThe badge collector now includes `'physics_critic'` in the `ManualReviewBadgeId` type and the collection logic reads `state?.physicsCritique`. The chain writes `physicsCritique: critique` to state. This looks correctly wired.\n\n### next.config.ts redirects\nNot shown in the excerpts, so I can't verify. I'll note this as unverifiable from provided code.\n\n### 41 knip-dead files archived\nThe knip.config.ts is shown and includes `enrich-state-with-suppliers.tsx` and `supplier-enrichment/**` as entry points. This looks correct.\n\n## Question 2: Did v5 introduce NEW gaps?\n\n### complianceGate not written to state\nAs identified above, `complianceGate` is computed but not written to `state.complianceGate`. The renderer reads it. This is a field written (by the chain's local variable) but not read (by the state consumer) — actually it's the opposite: it's a field READ by the renderer but NOT WRITTEN to state.\n\n### cost_reality not computed\nThe worker snapshot reads `parsed.cost_reality?.bom_total_gbp` and `parsed.cost_reality?.verdict`, and the badge collector references `state.cost_reality_rejection` and `state.cost_reality_status`. But no code computes these. Fields read but not written.\n\n### g3ManualReview not computed\nSame pattern — renderer and worker read `state.g3ManualReview` and `state.g3_review_gaps`, but no code writes them.\n\n### g1bManualReview not written\nThe badge collector reads `state?.g1bManualReview` but the chain never writes this field. The compliance gate result doesn't include a `g1bManualReview` boolean.\n\n### physicsCritique issues field\nThe badge collector checks `pcr.issues` with `i.severity === 'high'`, but I need to verify the `CritiqueReport` type from `physics-critic.ts` actually has an `issues` array with `severity` field. The import shows `type CritiqueReport` from `physics-critic`. Without seeing that file, I can't confirm the shape matches. But the chain writes `physicsCritique: critique` and the renderer reads `state?.physicsCritique.issues` — if the CritiqueReport type has `issues`, this is fine.\n\n### state.complianceGate vs local variable\nThe chain stores the result in local `complianceGate` but the state object doesn't include `complianceGate: complianceGate`. This means the renderer's `state?.complianceGate` will always be undefined, so the G1b badge will never fire.\n\n### Worker snapshot reads fields that don't exist\nThe worker reads `parsed.cost_reality?.bom_total_gbp` and `parsed.cost_reality?.verdict` — these will always be null since the chain never writes `cost_reality` to state. Not a crash (optional chaining), but the diagnostic snapshot is always empty for these fields.\n\n### M6 race condition\nThe `recoverStaleRunningRows()` function does a select then individual updates. Between the select and update, another worker could claim or recover the same row. The `.eq('status', 'running')` CAS guard on the update prevents double-recovery, but the select could return stale data. This is acceptable for a recovery mechanism.\n\nActually, there's a subtle issue: Part A uses `.neq('worker_id', WORKER_ID)` to exclude the current worker. But if the current worker has a running job (from `processJob`), and the worker crashes and restarts, the new worker process has a DIFFERENT `WORKER_ID` (it includes PID). So the old running job's `worker_id` won't match the new `WORKER_ID`, and it WILL be recovered. That's correct behavior.\n\nBut wait — what if two worker processes are running on the same host? The `.like('worker_id', `${hostPrefix}%`)` would match both. The `.neq('worker_id', WORKER_ID)` excludes only the current process. If there's a legitimate second worker on the same host, its running jobs would be incorrectly recovered. This is probably fine since the design assumes one worker per host.\n\n### M8 state.json upload race\nThe worker uploads state.json after the chain completes. But the chain writes state.json, then Engine B/C/D read and rewrite it. The worker reads the final state.json after all engines complete. This is correct.\n\n## Question 3: Is the chain now DRIFT-PROOF?\n\nCan a future session accidentally introduce a parallel engine?\n\nLooking at the architecture:\n- The chain is `serial-design-chain-v2.tsx` — a single script\n- The worker calls `npx tsx scripts/serial-design-chain-v2.tsx`\n- There's no mechanism preventing someone from creating a `serial-design-chain-v3.tsx` or adding a new route that calls different stages\n\nThe knip.config.ts helps detect dead code, but it doesn't prevent new entry points from being added. There's no:\n- Central registry of chain stages\n- Type-level enforcement that all state fields are populated\n- CI check that the worker's expected state fields match what the chain produces\n- Documentation of the state contract\n\nThe state object is typed as `any` in the renderer — there's no shared type definition that both the chain (writer) and renderer (reader) must satisfy. This means a future session could add a field to the chain but forget to update the renderer, or vice versa.\n\nSpecifically:\n- No shared `EngineState` type that both chain and renderer import\n- No compile-time check that all fields the renderer reads are written by the chain\n- No integration test that validates state shape end-to-end\n- The worker's state_snapshot_json extraction is manually kept in sync with the chain's state shape\n\n## Question 4: OPEN items still deferred\n\n### variation_of parent inheritance\nThe worker code shows `variation_of` and `variation_label` fields on the job, but the chain doesn't use them. The comment says \"The API already pre-composes the child brief at submit time, so reading variation_label is only used for logging clarity here.\" But if the parent's state (module decomposition, research, etc.) isn't inherited by the child, each variation starts from scratch — no incremental refinement. This means variations are expensive (full chain run each time) and can't build on the parent's work.\n\nIs this critical? If variations are meant to be independent explorations, it's fine. If they're meant to be incremental tweaks, it's a gap. Given the cost of each run (60-90 min, £0.50-2.00 in LLM tokens), not inheriting parent state makes variations impractical for anything beyond 2-3 alternatives.\n\n### in-PDF physicsCritique section\nThe badge collector now fires the `physics_critic` badge, but I need to check if there's a full render section (cover-strip + appendix entry) as claimed. The badge collector code shows the appendix content, so this seems wired. But the claim says \"Cover-strip + appendix entry with scores + high-severity findings\" — the badge does surface in the cover strip (that's what badges do) and the appendix. So this seems complete.\n\nActually, looking more carefully at the v5 claims: \"physicsCritique render section — new 'physics_critic' badge ID in collectManualReviewBadges. Cover-strip + appendix entry with scores + high-severity findings.\" This is just the badge, not a dedicated render section. The badge surfaces in the cover strip and appendix. That's probably sufficient, but it's not a \"section\" — it's a badge. If the physics critique has detailed scores and findings that deserve their own page or section, a badge alone might not be enough.\n\n### Other deferred items\nWithout seeing the full list of deferred items, I can only comment on what's mentioned. The variation_of inheritance seems like the most critical gap because it makes the variation feature essentially unusable for practical purposes.\n\n## Question 5: Engine D OPENROUTER key path\n\nThe chain spawns Engine D via:\n```typescript\nexecFileSync('npx', ['tsx', resolve(__dirname, 'enrich-state-with-suppliers.tsx'), statePath, '--write'], {\n    stdio: 'inherit',\n    cwd: resolve(__dirname, '..'),\n})\n```\n\nThe worker loads env files including:\n```javascript\nresolve(homedir(), '.claude/secrets/openrouter.env'),\n```\n\nBut the v5 description says Engine D's \"own env bootstrap loads OPENROUTER from /Users/tristanfischer/secrets/openrouter.env (note: NOT ~/.claude/secrets/).\"\n\nSo the question is: does `enrich-state-with-suppliers.tsx` have its own env bootstrap that loads from a different path?\n\nThe worker passes `env: process.env` to the child process (via `spawn` in `runChild`, but the chain uses `execFileSync` with `env: process.env`). Wait, actually the chain's `execFileSync` call doesn't explicitly pass `env: process.env` — it uses the default which inherits the parent's env.\n\nBut the worker sets up the env before spawning the chain. So the chain inherits the worker's env, which includes OPENROUTER_API_KEY from `~/.claude/secrets/openrouter.env`. Then the chain spawns Engine D, which inherits the chain's env (which includes the key).\n\nHowever, if `enrich-state-with-suppliers.tsx` has its OWN env bootstrap that tries to load from `/Users/tristanfischer/secrets/openrouter.env` and that file doesn't exist, AND the script treats the missing file as a fatal error rather than falling back to the inherited env... then Engine D would fail.\n\nBut looking at the worker's `loadEnvFile` function, it only sets env vars that aren't already set (`if (!process.env[k]) process.env[k] = v`). So if OPENROUTER_API_KEY is already in the env from the worker's bootstrap, even if Engine D's own bootstrap tries to load from the wrong path and fails, the key would still be available from the inherited env.\n\nThe risk is: if Engine D's bootstrap OVERRIDES the env var or exits fatally when the file is missing. Without seeing Engine D's code, I can't confirm. But based on the pattern of `loadEnvFile` (which only sets if not already present), and assuming Engine D uses a similar pattern, the inherited key would be used.\n\nHowever, if Engine D's bootstrap uses a DIFFERENT loadEnvFile that doesn't have the `if (!process.env[k])` guard, it could clear the key. Or if it exits with a fatal error when the file is missing.\n\nThe real question is: does it matter? If the key is already in env from the worker bootstrap, Engine D will work regardless of its own bootstrap. If the key is NOT in env (e.g., running the chain directly without the worker), and Engine D's bootstrap looks in the wrong path, then Engine D silently degrades.\n\nGiven that the worker is the primary production path, and the worker loads the key from the correct path, this probably doesn't matter for production. But for local development (running the chain directly), it could be an issue.\n\nLet me now compile my findings into a numbered list.\n\nActually, let me re-examine the state object more carefully. The excerpt shows:\n\n```typescript\nconst state = {\n    projectId: 'chain-v2-' + Date.now(),\n    parsedBrief: parsedResult.data,\n    moduleDecomposition: design,\n    naturalLanguageLayer: nl,\n    briefOverviewProse: design.brief_overview_prose ?? null,\n    keyMetrics: keyMetrics ?? null,\n    brief: briefBlock,\n    designDecisions,\n    partVerifications,\n    partRecommendations,\n    partVerificationSummary: { ... },\n    physicsCritique: critique,\n    physicsLedger,\n    gatesPassed: allPassed,\n    grammarVerdicts: ...,\n    g5M